---
title: "X25519: One Ladder, Two Public Keys, One Shared Secret"
author: celaltokmak61
date: 2026-11-27
abstract: A hands-on guide to stdlib/x25519.ltl — scalar clamping, the Montgomery ladder, base-point derivation, and the RFC 7748 section 5.2 test vector pinned as executable Lateralus tests.
---

## The pitch

X25519 is the key exchange behind TLS 1.3, WireGuard, Signal, age, and SSH. The whole protocol fits one idea: clamp a 32-byte scalar, walk a Montgomery ladder over Curve25519, and read the shared u-coordinate back as 32 little-endian bytes.

```lateralus
import stdlib.x25519

let alice_pub = stdlib.x25519.derive_public(alice_secret)
let shared = stdlib.x25519.scalar_mult(alice_secret, bob_pub)
```

The Lateralus implementation uses arbitrary-precision integers. It is correct for ordinary key agreement but is **not constant-time**, so keep it out of adversarial side-channel environments.

## Clamping is not optional

RFC 7748 section 5 says a scalar must be decoded as a little-endian integer, then clamped:

- clear the low three bits (`s[0] &= 248`) so the scalar is a multiple of the cofactor 8;
- clear the high bit (`s[31] &= 127`) so it stays below 2^255;
- set the next-high bit (`s[31] |= 64`) so the scalar always has the top bit set.

`clamp_scalar` does exactly that. Skipping it is the most common hand-rolled X25519 bug: your public key will still look like 32 bytes, but it will not match any other implementation.

Pinned vector (RFC 7748 §5.2, Alice's private scalar):

```lateralus
@test
fn clamp_matches_rfc7748() -> bool {
    let secret = [119, 7, 109, 10, 115, 24, 165, 125, 60, 22, 193, 114,
                  81, 178, 102, 69, 223, 76, 47, 135, 235, 192, 153, 42,
                  177, 119, 251, 165, 29, 185, 44, 42]
    let clamped = stdlib.x25519.clamp_scalar(secret)
    return clamped[0] == 120 && clamped[31] == 64
}
```

## The ladder

`scalar_mult(scalar, u_coord)` walks 255 ladder steps, from bit 254 down to bit 0. At each step it conditionally swaps `(x2, x3)` and `(z2, z3)`, then performs one differential addition-and-doubling. The result is `x2 * inv(z2) mod 2^255-19`, written back as 32 little-endian bytes.

The `cswap` helper returns `[b, a]` when the swap bit is 1 and `[a, b]` otherwise. It is a plain branch in this implementation — correct, but not a constant-time select.

## Base point derivation

Alice's public key is `scalar_mult(secret, [9, 0, ..., 0])`. The base point u-coordinate is 9; `derive_public` is a convenience wrapper that builds the 32-byte list for you.

Pinned vectors (RFC 7748 §5.2):

```lateralus
@test
fn derive_public_alice() -> bool {
    let secret = [119, 7, 109, 10, 115, 24, 165, 125, 60, 22, 193, 114,
                  81, 178, 102, 69, 223, 76, 47, 135, 235, 192, 153, 42,
                  177, 119, 251, 165, 29, 185, 44, 42]
    let expected = [133, 32, 240, 9, 137, 48, 167, 84, 116, 139, 125, 220,
                    180, 62, 247, 90, 13, 191, 58, 13, 38, 56, 26, 244,
                    235, 164, 169, 142, 170, 155, 78, 106]
    return stdlib.x25519.derive_public(secret) == expected
}

@test
fn derive_public_bob() -> bool {
    let secret = [93, 171, 8, 126, 98, 74, 138, 75, 121, 225, 127, 139,
                  131, 128, 14, 230, 111, 59, 177, 41, 38, 24, 182, 253,
                  28, 47, 139, 39, 255, 136, 224, 235]
    let expected = [222, 158, 219, 125, 123, 125, 193, 180, 211, 91, 97,
                    194, 236, 228, 53, 55, 63, 131, 67, 200, 91, 120,
                    103, 77, 173, 252, 126, 20, 111, 136, 43, 79]
    return stdlib.x25519.derive_public(secret) == expected
}
```

## The shared secret

Both sides arrive at the same 32 bytes. Alice multiplies her secret by Bob's public key; Bob multiplies his secret by Alice's public key. The modular inverse in the ladder is Fermat's little theorem: `a^(p-2) mod p` with `p = 2^255-19`.

Pinned vector (RFC 7748 §5.2):

```lateralus
@test
fn shared_secret_matches_from_both_sides() -> bool {
    let alice_secret = [119, 7, 109, 10, 115, 24, 165, 125, 60, 22, 193,
                        114, 81, 178, 102, 69, 223, 76, 47, 135, 235,
                        192, 153, 42, 177, 119, 251, 165, 29, 185, 44, 42]
    let bob_public = [222, 158, 219, 125, 123, 125, 193, 180, 211, 91, 97,
                      194, 236, 228, 53, 55, 63, 131, 67, 200, 91, 120,
                      103, 77, 173, 252, 126, 20, 111, 136, 43, 79]
    let expected = [74, 93, 157, 91, 164, 206, 45, 225, 114, 142, 59, 244,
                    128, 53, 15, 37, 224, 126, 33, 201, 71, 209, 158, 51,
                    118, 240, 155, 60, 30, 22, 23, 66]
    return stdlib.x25519.scalar_mult(alice_secret, bob_public) == expected
}
```

## Runnable example

Save as `examples/x25519_tutorial.ltl` and run `lateralus test examples/x25519_tutorial.ltl`.

```lateralus
import stdlib.x25519

@test
fn alice_public_key() -> bool {
    let secret = [119, 7, 109, 10, 115, 24, 165, 125, 60, 22, 193, 114,
                  81, 178, 102, 69, 223, 76, 47, 135, 235, 192, 153, 42,
                  177, 119, 251, 165, 29, 185, 44, 42]
    let expected = [133, 32, 240, 9, 137, 48, 167, 84, 116, 139, 125, 220,
                    180, 62, 247, 90, 13, 191, 58, 13, 38, 56, 26, 244,
                    235, 164, 169, 142, 170, 155, 78, 106]
    return stdlib.x25519.derive_public(secret) == expected
}

@test
fn bob_public_key() -> bool {
    let secret = [93, 171, 8, 126, 98, 74, 138, 75, 121, 225, 127, 139,
                  131, 128, 14, 230, 111, 59, 177, 41, 38, 24, 182, 253,
                  28, 47, 139, 39, 255, 136, 224, 235]
    let expected = [222, 158, 219, 125, 123, 125, 193, 180, 211, 91, 97,
                    194, 236, 228, 53, 55, 63, 131, 67, 200, 91, 120,
                    103, 77, 173, 252, 126, 20, 111, 136, 43, 79]
    return stdlib.x25519.derive_public(secret) == expected
}

@test
fn shared_secret() -> bool {
    let alice_secret = [119, 7, 109, 10, 115, 24, 165, 125, 60, 22, 193,
                        114, 81, 178, 102, 69, 223, 76, 47, 135, 235,
                        192, 153, 42, 177, 119, 251, 165, 29, 185, 44, 42]
    let bob_public = [222, 158, 219, 125, 123, 125, 193, 180, 211, 91, 97,
                      194, 236, 228, 53, 55, 63, 131, 67, 200, 91, 120,
                      103, 77, 173, 252, 126, 20, 111, 136, 43, 79]
    let expected = [74, 93, 157, 91, 164, 206, 45, 225, 114, 142, 59, 244,
                    128, 53, 15, 37, 224, 126, 33, 201, 71, 209, 158, 51,
                    118, 240, 155, 60, 30, 22, 23, 66]
    return stdlib.x25519.scalar_mult(alice_secret, bob_public) == expected
}
```

Expected output:

```
3/3 tests passed
```

## When not to use it

Do not use this module where an attacker can measure timing or power. `cswap` branches, and the big-integer multiplications are variable-time. Use a vetted constant-time library for that. For learning, prototyping, and interoperability checks against RFC 7748 vectors, the Lateralus version is ideal.
